<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hans Kelsen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hans Kelsen was an Austrian jurist and legal philosopher, the author of the Pure Theory of Law, which treated law as a hierarchy of norms separate from politics and morals. He was the principal drafter of the Austrian federal constitution of 1920, which created the first specialised constitutional court, on which he served. Driven out by antisemitism, he emigrated and taught at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Berkeley, from 1942.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hans-kelsen.jpg" alt="Hans Kelsen" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Fayer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Kelsen_(1881%E2%80%931973)_~1930_%C2%A9_Georg_Fayer_(1892%E2%80%931950)_OeNB_8026867.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
